hey i'm dion robon i'm a markets  reporter for the wall street journal and  i want to talk to you a little bit about  a story that i think hasn't been getting  nearly enough attention that's about the  rising value of the dollar the dollar  rose by seven percent last year that was  its strongest year since 2015. so here's  why the dollar is rising and how that  increase in the value of the dollar  could have a major impact on the value  of the stock market let's get into it so  why is the dollar getting stronger  well it starts with the rebound that  we've seen in the u.s economy in 2021  the us added around 6 million jobs the  other big piece the economic puzzle is  gross domestic product gdp usgdp is  expected to grow around six percent for  2021 that's the best year we've had in  quite some time now all that matters to  the dollar because the value of the  dollar is reflective of again the us  economy people want to put their money  in a place where they know it's going to  be safe and a place in an economy that  they think is going to grow and when  more people want to put money in dollars  it makes the value of the dollar rise  having a strong economy is great but  what really draws the value of foreign  money to the us and the u.s dollar  is high treasury yields now i know what  you're thinking the u.s treasury yields  are historically low but if we compare  them to other industrialized and  developed nations well the value of u.s  treasury yields is very high that drives  a lot of money from outside the u.s into  the u.s when i talk to one of my  long-time sources john doyle he's the  vice president of trading and dealing at  tempest inc he tells me that the biggest  factor has been the hawkish turn by the  federal reserve to fight persistent  inflation here in the u.s the fed is  expected to hike interest rates faster  than most other central banks this year  that's going to in theory at least raise  the value of u.s treasury yields and  make the dollar even more attractive as  a place for foreigners to store their  money in the u.s so why could this be a  problem for the stock market  well let's think about it experts agree  corporate profits are the biggest driver  of stock prices and u.s companies have  increased their overseas presence in  recent years and in fact s p 500  companies generated 40 percent of their  revenue from outside the us according to  factset now those other countries  they've got other currencies and when  the value of the dollar rises the value  of those currencies declines now usually  prices stay pretty stable so if the  price of something isn't rising  but its value in dollar terms is  declining  that means companies are making less  money think about it like this if  something cost 100 euros and you were  getting 120 for every euro but now  you're getting 110 well that thing is  still being sold for 100 euros but you  as an american company are only getting  a hundred ten dollars  that's a loss this is what happens when  the value of the dollar rises the  dollar's seven percent gain in 2021  basically reversed its entire loss for  2020 so if we're back where we were in  2019 we're back at a place where the  strong dollar is hurting companies and  hurting international sales lower sales  equals lower profits which could drag  down stock prices if this continues into  2022 you can expect to hear more  executives complaining about the strong  dollar now add to that supply chain  bottlenecks inflation and a federal  reserve that instead of cutting rates  and making conditions easier is making  things tighter and you can see why the  strong dollar could have a real impact  on the stock market  if you enjoyed this video make sure and  hit the like button and if you're  interested in markets and you want to  watch and learn more be sure and  subscribe to keep up with my latest as  the west coast rapper sugarfree once  said if you stay ready you ain't got to  get ready  thanks for watching i'll see you next  time
 

